+  "addressing": "No explicit \"root\"/mount/registration rule is stated in this tip \u2014 the {?} root and the owner's \"connection\" statement post-date it (both are in pyto/questions.md, produced 2026-09-09; this tip is 2026-09-07). What exists is the address CONVENTION pyto later inherited almost verbatim: a hard `fn.` prefix enforced in code for Calculations \u2014 `if not self.address.startswith(\"fn.\"): raise ValueError(\"Calculation address must start with 'fn.'\")` (6309ff1:packages/quick_anno_py/chainspot_quick_anno/core.py:33-34) \u2014 and two informal, unenforced namespaces used by convention only: `px.*` for Parts seeded from real production material (`Part('px.tees.rings')`, `Part('px.badges.objects')` \u2014 6309ff1:experiments/quick-anno-python/s3.py:28-31, 6309ff1:experiments/quick-anno-python/s1.py:24-26) and `scratch.*` for Python-owned derived/investigation-only Parts (`Part('scratch.s3.ringLedger')`, `Part('scratch.s1.ownershipSummary')` \u2014 same lines). Every address segment after the prefix discriminates by Stage and concept (`px.tees.rings` vs `px.tees.family` vs `px.tees.px` vs `px.tees`, `px.badges.objects` vs `px.badges.px` vs `px.badges.muted`) \u2014 none of the roots are generic; each PCR is itself named `<Stage>.quick-anno` (`PCR('S3.quick-anno')`, `PCR('S1.quick-anno')`) and each Tick is named for what it does (`'InspectOwnership'`, `'AccountRings'`, `'CheckBalance'`), never a generic label. \"No registration\" for Parts is literal: `PxC.set` just writes into a plain dict keyed by address string (6309ff1:packages/quick_anno_py/chainspot_quick_anno/core.py:62-66, `_values: dict[str, Any]`), no schema, no namespace object, no mount step. The one place addresses ARE resolved/rewritten automatically is `PCR.calc`: if a bound Part's address already has a registered writer in this PCR, the binding is silently rewritten from a Part reference to a `ResultRef` to that writer's invocation id \u2014 \"if isinstance(source, Part) and source.address in self._writers: source = ResultRef(self._writers[source.address])\" (6309ff1:packages/quick_anno_py/chainspot_quick_anno/pcr.py:113-116) \u2014 so within one PCR, an address always resolves to its most recent producer rather than a stale store read; this is pyto's identical `{?} SilentRules` mechanism, present here already, unlabelled as a question. Missing in this tip: any statement of why the root should \"carry connection\" (that's the owner's later, 2026-09-09 framing) and any prefix beyond `px.`/`scratch.`/`fn.` (no `data.users`, no per-user or cross-project addressing \u2014 TableScope/CrossProjectReuse do not exist yet here).",
+  "viewer_and_pcr_render": "There is no Tick-keyed viewer here \u2014 confirming tick-observability-ledger row 3's classification (\"Neon correctness sheet... keyed by Stage, e.g. `s3:CroppedImage`... not by Tick\"). What a person sees is one PNG contact-sheet PER STAGE PER COURSE, `s3-neon-correctness-sheet.png`, built by `sheet(panels)` compositing named `panel()`s top-to-bottom (6309ff1:experiments/quick-anno-python/materialize_s3_neon.py:100-131), fed not from `PcrRun` at all but directly from the raw Node-bridge `snapshot.json` dict (rings/family/tees arrays) plus the canonical raster image \u2014 the Python PCR's `results`/testimony are NOT what the sheet renders from; the sheet is a second, independent consumer of the same snapshot the PCR also consumes (6309ff1:experiments/quick-anno-python/s3.py:25-38 vs 6309ff1:experiments/quick-anno-python/materialize_s3_neon.py:44-53). S3 grew the sheet from 9 to 11 panels specifically because full-panel bounding boxes could not answer \"what is this object\", only \"where is it\": \"panels 1-7 prove where each object was accepted and never what it is. Identity precedes geometry; a full-course panel cannot establish identity at course zoom\" (6309ff1:experiments/quick-anno-python/S3_CHECKPOINT.md:110-112), which is why `neon.crops` was added \u2014 a contact strip of one upscaled, subject-marked crop per object (6309ff1:packages/quick_anno_py/chainspot_quick_anno/neon.py:103-116, \"Identity precedes geometry: a full-course panel proves where an object was accepted, never what it is. This answers the second question.\"). Values shown are literal pixels/boxes drawn on the canonical raster, colour-coded per rejection bucket (grey=diamond-dropped, red=Badge-muted, orange=unframed, magenta=voted-out, green=accepted \u2014 6309ff1:experiments/quick-anno-python/materialize_s3_neon.py:100-127); no scalar/JSON Part value is rendered in the sheet. Separately, `PCR.mermaid()` DOES render per-Tick, per-Calculation structure as a picture (subgraph-per-Tick, node-per-invocation, edges labelled by argument name \u2014 6309ff1:packages/quick_anno_py/chainspot_quick_anno/pcr.py:179-219, near-identical to what tick-observability-ledger row 25 cites in pyto's own pcr.py), but it is explicitly NOT the pattern's spine: \"Not part of the pattern's spine: nothing consumes the .mmd, and a two-node graph needs no diagram. The emitter stays because the day a Stage graph has a dozen nodes it will be the cheapest way to see it\" (6309ff1:packages/quick_anno_py/chainspot_quick_anno/investigation.py:91-93), and both call sites leave it commented out (`# inv.write_mermaid(pcr) renders the composition when a graph needs a picture.` \u2014 6309ff1:experiments/quick-anno-python/s3.py:104). So today: the picture a person actually opens (the neon sheet) is Stage-keyed and value-bearing but PCR-blind; the picture that is Tick-keyed and PCR-shaped (Mermaid) is structure-only, value-blind, and unused. Nothing in this tip resembles \"PCR is Kibana\" as a render-definition abstraction \u2014 that framing is pyto's later (2026-09-09) Reframing 4, not present here; each Stage's materializer is a bespoke hand-written script, exactly the row-3/row-30 pattern the ledger critiques (\"no shared engine\").",
+  "storage": "No backend, no DuckDB, no sqlite, no content addressing anywhere in this tip (grep for all three across `experiments/quick-anno-python` and `packages/quick_anno_py` returns zero hits). `PxC` is a bare in-process Python dict: `self._values: dict[str, Any] = {}` and `self._calculations: dict[str, Calculation[Any, Any]] = {}` (6309ff1:packages/quick_anno_py/chainspot_quick_anno/core.py:49-51) \u2014 it dies with the process; nothing is retained, replayed, or hashed as a Part identity. The only sha256 in the whole diff verifies the raw corpus INPUT file's bytes against its Git-LFS pointer before running (\"Input verified by content, not by filename... sha256 e6616738640eee1d05d8cc2b59dd657f24b4a5a01dc2f68571b7e44c6f372b8a\" \u2014 6309ff1:experiments/quick-anno-python/S3_VALIDATION.md:17-23) \u2014 an external provenance check, not a PxC key. What IS \"archived,\" and why: every run's Node-bridge-exported `snapshot.json`, the Python-derived `python-summary.json`, the production `s3.receipt.txt`, the Mermaid `.mmd`, and every neon PNG are written as plain files under `experiments/quick-anno-python/generated/<Course>-<Stage>/` (named in 6309ff1:experiments/quick-anno-python/export_s3_snapshot.cjs:138-140 and 6309ff1:experiments/quick-anno-python/S3_VALIDATION.md:129-150) \u2014 but `.gitignore` explicitly EXCLUDES that whole `generated/` tree from the repo (\"quick_anno Python investigation outputs \u2014 regenerated by experiments/quick-anno-python/s<N>.py, never committed\" \u2014 6309ff1:.gitignore, added this diff), so nothing here is retained across sessions; it is scratch-disk output for one interactive run, reproduced fresh every time the repro command is rerun. This is strictly earlier than pyto's Day 3 target (a durable, content-addressed, per-user materials store) \u2014 this tip has neither the content-addressing nor the durability half of that thesis yet.",
+  "materialization_and_ticks": "`PCR.run(pxc)` executes every Tick's Invocations in declaration order and returns a `PcrRun(pcr, ticks, results)` (6309ff1:packages/quick_anno_py/chainspot_quick_anno/pcr.py:135-177) \u2014 the exact `PrincipleComponentRender -> Tick[] -> Calculation[]` shape from origin.md, one generation before pyto's own copy. `results: dict[str, Any]` holds every invocation's actual computed value keyed by invocation id (line 136, 162) \u2014 so, as tick-observability-ledger section 3 notes for pyto's later version, the values ARE live in memory at the end of a run \u2014 but `CalculationTestimony` (what gets serialized) carries only `id, calculation, inputs, args, into` (6309ff1:packages/quick_anno_py/chainspot_quick_anno/pcr.py:60-65) with `inputs` as `px:`/`fn:` address-ref strings only (line 149, 157) \u2014 no value, no digest, no duration field exists in this tip at all (this predates pyto's Day-2-style `observations`/`Receipt` seam entirely). S3 is flagged as the first Stage to actually exercise Tick-to-Tick composition inside one PCR: \"S3 is the first Stage with something to compose: the second consumes the first's result directly inside the same PCR\" (6309ff1:experiments/quick-anno-python/s3.py:7-8), concretely `accountRings`'s `into=ledger` Part becomes `checkBalance`'s `ledger=ledger` binding, which `PCR.calc`'s writer-rewrite (pcr.py:113-116) turns into a `fn:accountRings` ResultRef rather than a `px:` store read \u2014 Mermaid shows this as `accountRings -->|ledger| checkBalance` (6309ff1:experiments/quick-anno-python/S3_CHECKPOINT.md:90-91). Materialization is verified by a hand-authored accounting invariant rather than by inspecting values directly: `checkBalance`'s `balanced` field asserts four chained equalities so \"every enclosed ring left through exactly one named door\" (6309ff1:experiments/quick-anno-python/S3_CHECKPOINT.md:70-80; the Calculation itself at 6309ff1:experiments/quick-anno-python/s3.py:56-71) \u2014 `balanced: true` is the pass/fail signal for the whole investigation, and it held on all six Dev6 courses (6309ff1:experiments/quick-anno-python/DEV6_S3_SURVEY.md:26-40, \"balanced: true on all six\"). No entry point runs `observe`-equivalent tracing by default; the materializer is invoked as a separate subprocess against the raw snapshot, not against `PcrRun` (6309ff1:packages/quick_anno_py/chainspot_quick_anno/investigation.py:99-104), so pyto's still-open gap #6 (\"No entry point... `s3.py` neither passes `observe` nor calls the Mermaid writer\") is visible here in its precursor form too.",
+  "receipts_and_telemetry": "Two distinct, unrelated things are called \"receipt\" in this tip, and neither is telemetry (zero hits for \"telemetry\" anywhere). (1) Production's own plain-text receipt, `s3.receipt.txt`, written verbatim from the TS `@chainspot/alg` runtime's `receiptText` and saved by the Node export bridge (6309ff1:experiments/quick-anno-python/export_s3_snapshot.cjs:139, `s3.receiptText`) \u2014 human-readable counter lines like \"enclosed holes: 32 / elongated rings: 27 / ... / Tee objects: 18\" (6309ff1:experiments/quick-anno-python/S3_CHECKPOINT.md:37-49). This is the artifact pyto's ledger row 3 already cites as `pyto/reference/chainspot-quick-anno/s3.py:90` calling `pcr.run(pxc)` with production's receipt as an input, not something PxC produces. (2) The claims ledger's use of \"Receipt:\" as a citation label in `docs/CLAIMS-LEDGER.md` rows 31-35 (added this diff, 6309ff1:docs/CLAIMS-LEDGER.md, e.g. row 33's Receipt column pointing at `experiments/quick-anno-python/generated/<Course>-S3/s3-tee-crops.png` and `DEV6_S3_SURVEY.md`) \u2014 an evidence-citation convention, not a data structure. Neither corresponds to pyto's later `Receipt` dataclass (address + implementation_sha256 + declared/actual consumes + writes + shadowed_inputs) \u2014 that does not exist anywhere in this tip; `CalculationTestimony` here has no digest, duration, or receipt field at all (see materialization_and_ticks). No dashboards, metrics, counters, or any telemetry/observability-as-a-product concept appear; the only \"observability\" delivered is the one-shot neon sheet plus the printed `balanced` JSON.",
