+  "addressing": "No explicit root, mount, or registration table exists anywhere in this tip; addressing is entirely convention plus two hard rules. Rule 1 (enforced): a Calculation address must start with \"fn.\" \u2014 `if not self.address.startswith(\"fn.\"): raise ValueError(\"Calculation address must start with 'fn.'\")` (5c87d48:packages/quick_anno_py/chainspot_quick_anno/core.py:33-34). Rule 2 (enforced): one writer per Part address inside a composition \u2014 `if prior is not None: raise ValueError(f\"PCR '{self.name}' has multiple writers for '{output.address}'\")` (5c87d48:packages/quick_anno_py/chainspot_quick_anno/pcr.py:120-123), proven by `test_duplicate_writer_rejected` (5c87d48:packages/quick_anno_py/tests/test_first_class.py:38-51). Everything else is a flat string-keyed map with no prefix validation on Parts at all \u2014 `Part.__post_init__` only checks non-empty (5c87d48:packages/quick_anno_py/chainspot_quick_anno/core.py:17-19) \u2014 and the production JS store is the same shape: `PxC's browser-safe address space. ExecBoard remains as a compatibility name because this is the existing production board evolving in place, not a second synchronized store.` (5c87d48:packages/alg/src/exec/board.ts:38-40), keyed by an intentionally open `SlotRef = string` \u2014 `Deliberately a plain string, not a closed enum: R2 requires operations at a finer grain than the existing engine units, so the slot namespace has to stay open ... without this file growing every time a unit gets decomposed further.` (5c87d48:packages/alg/src/exec/contract.ts:15-23). Grep across the whole tip for \"mount\" inside experiments/, packages/quick_anno_py/, src/lib/evidence-workbench/, and packages/alg/src/exec/ returned zero hits \u2014 the term does not exist in this branch. There is no {?}-style root here either; this tip predates and does not reference pyto's `{?}` convention.\n\nThe one working instance of the owner's connection principle is a namespace split the experiments actually use, unenforced by any code but followed consistently across two stages: production-mirrored facts live under `px.badges.*` / `px.baskets.*` (e.g. `Part(\"px.badges.objects\")`, `Part(\"px.baskets.family\")`, 5c87d48:experiments/quick-anno-python/s1.py:33-35 and 5c87d48:experiments/quick-anno-python/s2.py:33-36), while Python-derived investigation output is deliberately segregated under a different root segment \u2014 `Part(\"scratch.s1.ownershipSummary\")` and `Part(\"scratch.s2.basketSummary\")` (5c87d48:experiments/quick-anno-python/s1.py:36,71 and 5c87d48:experiments/quick-anno-python/s2.py:37,75), read back later as \"The Python-owned derived Part `scratch.s1.ownershipSummary` produced...\" (5c87d48:experiments/quick-anno-python/S1_CHECKPOINT.md, see quotes). This root segment discriminates meaningfully \u2014 \"who owns this fact, production or the investigation\" \u2014 rather than being a generic label, and it is what lets a later PQL prefix query or viewer tell provenance apart without a separate registry. Calculation addresses nest the same way: `fn.quickAnno.s1.summarizeOwnership` / `fn.quickAnno.s2.summarizeBaskets` (5c87d48:experiments/quick-anno-python/s1.py:43, 5c87d48:experiments/quick-anno-python/s2.py:45) so the experiment name is itself part of the connective address. Note this convention is NOT present in the production JS SlotRef examples shown in contract.ts (`'badgeStage.brightMask'`, `'assignment.scoring'` \u2014 no `px.` prefix at all), so `px.`/`scratch.` is a Python-authoring-layer convention layered on top of a JS core that doesn't use it.",
+  "viewer_and_pcr_render": "The quick-anno-python experiment itself has no viewer: it emits a static PNG contact sheet via PIL and a `.mmd` Mermaid string per run (`(OUT / \"python-S1.mmd\").write_text(pcr.mermaid())`, 5c87d48:experiments/quick-anno-python/s1.py:73), i.e. exactly the same \"Mermaid + digest-only testimony, no rendered value\" gap pyto's own ledger names.\n\nBut the wider tip DOES contain a real, working per-Tick browser render \u2014 Storybook functioning literally as Kibana \u2014 under `src/lib/evidence-workbench/`. `TickInspection.svelte` renders, per Tick: an identity strip \u2014 `<dt>Run Args identity</dt><dd><code>{pcr.paramsHash ?? 'UNKNOWN'}</code></dd>` / `<dt>Plan</dt><dd><code>{pcr.planFingerprint}</code></dd>` / `<dt>Run result</dt><dd><code>{pcr.runResultId}</code></dd>` plus an explicit honesty line `<strong>{pcr.runResultIdentityScope}</strong> \u2014 {pcr.runResultIdentityLimitation}` (5c87d48:src/lib/evidence-workbench/TickInspection.svelte:40-56); \"Exact PxC inputs\" listing `tick.actualConsumes` with a \"Declared but unread\" warning when `declaredConsumes` and `actualConsumes` diverge (5c87d48:src/lib/evidence-workbench/TickInspection.svelte:59-67); \"Calculation identity\" showing `calculation.address`, `SHA-256 {calculation.implementationHash}`, and `<small class=\"warning\">Not transitive: {calculation.limitation}</small>` (5c87d48:src/lib/evidence-workbench/TickInspection.svelte:70-81); \"Exact PxC outputs\" listing `tick.writes` with `write.kind` (5c87d48:src/lib/evidence-workbench/TickInspection.svelte:83-97); and \"Materializations emitted by this Tick\" \u2014 each with `materialization.kind`, `materialization.id`, `SHA-256 {materialization.sha256}`, optional `{width}\u00d7{height}`, or else `No persisted Materialization at this boundary. PxC outputs remain inspectable by address.` (5c87d48:src/lib/evidence-workbench/TickInspection.svelte:100-119). A \"Residue / UNKNOWN\" section renders per-PCR pixel-accounting notes computed by the parent host (5c87d48:src/lib/evidence-workbench/TickInspection.svelte:122-133; residue logic in 5c87d48:src/lib/evidence-workbench/PcrInspectionHost.svelte:45-76).\n\n`PcrInspectionHost.svelte` feeds `TickInspection` a `pcr`/`tickId`/`residue`, and separately renders a pixel projection (`BadgeEvidenceView` / `M1EvidenceView`) selected by a `materializationView` prop, explicitly kept apart from computation state: `<strong>Projection-only MaterializationView</strong><span>View Args never cross the production gateway</span>` (5c87d48:src/lib/evidence-workbench/PcrInspectionHost.svelte:88-93) \u2014 i.e. how-you're-looking-at-it is not allowed to leak into what-happened. The delivery vehicle is confirmed as Storybook via a staging-only route: `/lab/pcr` reuses the Storybook inspection molecules against the same materialized computation. It opens with the checked `DEFAULT_PCR_INSPECTION_PLAN` (Badge PCR, `badgeStage.masks`, badge 1, composed Materialization). The staging build fails loudly if the corpus is absent or any default PCR, Tick, or specimen identity has drifted. (5c87d48:src/lib/evidence-workbench/README.md:27-30), gated so `the production build contains neither route nor its LAB code` (5c87d48:src/lib/evidence-workbench/README.md:24-25). Values are per Tick (via `checkpoint.testimony`), not per Stage \u2014 this is a Tick-granular Kibana, ahead of pyto's own S3-neon-sheet precedent which the ledger says is \"keyed by Stage, not Tick.\"",
+  "storage": "quick_anno_py's PxC is in-memory only \u2014 `self._values: dict[str, Any] = {}` (5c87d48:packages/quick_anno_py/chainspot_quick_anno/core.py:49-51) \u2014 with no persistence, no digesting of values, and no content addressing of Parts at all; `PxC.set` just replaces the dict entry and reports a write kind (5c87d48:packages/quick_anno_py/chainspot_quick_anno/core.py:62-66). The authoring-only `Pcr`/`graph.py` layer explicitly disclaims execution or storage ownership: \"This deliberately does not execute ChainSpot calculations. ... Execution remains owned by ChainSpot/PxC.\" (5c87d48:packages/quick_anno_py/chainspot_quick_anno/graph.py:29-33), and the README lists what is out of scope: \"Does not yet execute through PxC, materialize warm Parts, draw pixel annotations, emit existing PCR YAML byte-for-byte, or plug the generated Mermaid into a browser renderer.\" (5c87d48:experiments/quick-anno-python/README.md:43). No DuckDB reference exists anywhere in this tip (grep for \"duckdb\" across the whole extracted tree, case-insensitive, returned zero hits) \u2014 missing, not just unused in this experiment.\n\nWhat actually gets archived by the S1/S2 pipeline: a JSON snapshot tagged with a schema version, `schema: 'chainspot-quick-anno-s1-snapshot@1'`, plus the real Stage's own raw receipts written verbatim next to it \u2014 `fs.writeFileSync(path.join(outDir, 's0.receipt.txt'), s0.receiptText + '\\n'); fs.writeFileSync(path.join(outDir, 's1.receipt.txt'), s1.receiptText + '\\n');` (5c87d48:experiments/quick-anno-python/export_s1_snapshot.cjs:47-48,81-82). This is a one-shot export/archive for reproducibility and audit against the exact production run, not a running cache with hit counters \u2014 there is no hit/miss counter in quick_anno_py's PxC.\n\nThe one real content-addressed storage design in the tip lives in production JS, not in the quick-anno experiment: `ArtifactRef {id, kind, sha256, uri, dims?}` is a \"Content-addressed reference to an artifact the sink has stored. `uri` is sink-defined (a file:// path from the Node sink, an in-memory handle from a browser/collector sink) \u2014 the exec core never interprets it, only carries it through the receipt.\" (5c87d48:packages/alg/src/exec/contract.ts:91-95,104-112). Storage backend is chosen by an injected sink object (dependency injection), not by address-prefix dispatch, but the artifact's own declared `kind` is a closed union already in production \u2014 `'rgba' | 'mask' | 'scalarField' | 'orientationField' | 'componentSet' | 'candidateSet' | 'badgeEvidence' | 'm1Representation' | 'm2Representation' | 'polyline' | 'measurementTable'` (5c87d48:packages/alg/src/exec/contract.ts:79-90) \u2014 this is a concrete, shipping instance of \"a declared Part kind\" that pyto's own `{?} StorageKinds` question is still asking for permission to build.",
+  "materialization_and_ticks": "`PcrRun.results: dict[str, Any]` (5c87d48:packages/quick_anno_py/chainspot_quick_anno/pcr.py:78, populated at line 162: `results[invocation.id] = value`) holds every invocation's actual computed value in memory, keyed by invocation id \u2014 this independently confirms the ledger's central finding for pyto's own PCR (\"the actual computed Part value of every Calculation in every Tick is therefore live in the returned object\") is also true of this ChainSpot Python prototype, and just as unexploited: `CalculationTestimony` carries only `inputs: dict[str, str]` (a `\"px:address\"` or `\"fn:calc_id\"` tag, not a value), `args`, and `into` (5c87d48:packages/quick_anno_py/chainspot_quick_anno/pcr.py:59-66) \u2014 no value, no digest, ever leaves `results` for the testimony. There is likewise no generic materializer: `materialize_s1_neon.py` and `materialize_s2_neon.py` are hand-written per stage, reading a JSON snapshot directly rather than a `PcrRun` (5c87d48:experiments/quick-anno-python/materialize_s1_neon.py:113-150) \u2014 the same \"no `materialize_run(run, pxc)`\" gap the ledger names for pyto (tick-observability-ledger.md section 3, item 1).\n\n\"Neon first\" is stated as the explicit, repeated correctness rule governing every materialization in this tip: \"A subtle render is not accepted as the first correctness proof; **neon first** is the investigation convention.\" (5c87d48:experiments/quick-anno-python/S1_CHECKPOINT.md:101) and, more sharply, \"The first subtle contact sheet was not sufficiently judgeable. The accepted investigation convention is **neon first**: make semantic differences unmistakable before making a render aesthetically subtle.\" (5c87d48:experiments/quick-anno-python/S1_VALIDATION.md:70). The delegate checklist repeats it as step 7 of 10: \"materialize a **neon-first** visual correctness check\" followed by step 8, \"visually inspect the materialization before claiming success\" (5c87d48:experiments/quick-anno-python/S1_CHECKPOINT.md:119-120). This matches pyto's own neon.py house rule verbatim in spirit.\n\nOn Tick identity: production JS resolves the ledger's gap #5 (\"No Tick object identity in the output\") by fiat \u2014 a Tick literally IS its Receipt: \"A Tick is the existing gateway Receipt understood as inspection testimony: exact addresses in, frozen calculations, exact addresses out. It adds no execution authority and deliberately has no run() method.\" followed by `export type TickTestimony = Receipt;` (5c87d48:packages/alg/src/exec/contract.ts:142-148). `Receipt.opId` (5c87d48:packages/alg/src/exec/contract.ts:128) is therefore the stable per-run Tick id that pyto's ledger says nothing currently assigns. quick_anno_py's own `Tick` object, by contrast, is a plain `name` + `calculations: list[Invocation]` (5c87d48:packages/quick_anno_py/chainspot_quick_anno/pcr.py:28-31) with no stable id beyond the string name, so this experiment does not close that gap on its own \u2014 only production JS does.\n\nS2's validation explicitly frames this as a repeatable, mechanical transfer pattern now proven twice: \"This is now the second stage using the same transfer pattern. S3 should be mostly mechanical for a Luna: real Stage snapshot -> Python PxC Parts -> small PCR/PQL investigation -> neon-first materialization -> visually inspect -> emit Mermaid/testimony.\" (5c87d48:experiments/quick-anno-python/S2_VALIDATION.md:76).",
+  "receipts_and_telemetry": "quick_anno_py's own receipt shape is a strict subset of what pyto's pcr.py already has (per the ledger): `CalculationTestimony(id, calculation, inputs, args, into)`, `TickTestimony(name, calculations)`, `PcrRun(pcr, ticks, results)` (5c87d48:packages/quick_anno_py/chainspot_quick_anno/pcr.py:59-78) \u2014 no digest field, no duration, no writer-kind distinction beyond `PxWrite(address, kind)` in core.py with only two kinds: `kind = \"replacement\" if address in self._values else \"new-address\"` (5c87d48:packages/quick_anno_py/chainspot_quick_anno/core.py:63-64). This experiment is therefore earlier/smaller than pyto's own pcr.py, not a source to pull a richer shape from directly.\n\nThe richer, reusable shape is production JS's `Receipt`: `{opId, frozenCalculations: FrozenCalculation[], startedAtMs, durationMs, declaredConsumes, declaredProduces, actualConsumes, actualProduces, writes: PxWriteTestimony[], probes: Probe[], artifacts: ArtifactRef[]}` (5c87d48:packages/alg/src/exec/contract.ts:126-139), with `declared*`-vs-`actual*` framed as a conformance signal: \"The two diverging is a conformance failure the gateway surfaces rather than silently accepting.\" (5c87d48:packages/alg/src/exec/contract.ts:122-124). `FrozenCalculation` states its own hashing limitation as data, not just as documentation: `{address: 'fn.${string}', implementationHash: string /* SHA-256 of the bound executable function body */, identityScope: 'runtime-function-body', limitation: 'called helpers, constants, templates, and assets are not covered'}` (5c87d48:packages/alg/src/exec/contract.ts:61-67) \u2014 this is the exact same caveat pyto's own `{?} CacheInvalidation` question raises about `implementation_sha256` excluding helpers, arrived at independently on the production JS side and, unlike pyto, written directly into the receipt type rather than left to a side document. `PxWriteTestimony.kind` has three values in production \u2014 `'new-address' | 'refinement' | 'replacement'` (5c87d48:packages/alg/src/exec/contract.ts:71-73) \u2014 versus quick_anno_py's/pyto's two; `'refinement'` has no equivalent on the Python side anywhere in this tip. `Probe {name: string, value: number}` (5c87d48:packages/alg/src/exec/contract.ts:116-119) is a minimal named-numeric-telemetry primitive with no equivalent field in quick_anno_py's testimony. `ArtifactRef` (documented under storage above) is the telemetry-facing content-addressed artifact shape actually rendered by the viewer (`materialization.sha256`, `materialization.dims`, 5c87d48:src/lib/evidence-workbench/TickInspection.svelte:104-112).",
