+  "addressing": "No explicit root/mount/namespace-registration rule exists in this tip, and the owner's \"connection\" framing (dated 2026-09-09, two days after this 2026-09-07 commit) is not present here \u2014 recording that as missing rather than inferring it. What the code actually enforces: Part identity is address-only and unconstrained beyond non-empty (core.py:17-19, `\"\"\"A semantic PxC address. Identity is the address, not the current value.\"\"\"` at core.py:13) \u2014 any string is a legal Part address, so \"px.\" vs \"scratch.\" vs anything else is pure convention, never checked. Calculation addresses are the one enforced prefix rule: `if not self.address.startswith(\"fn.\"): raise ValueError(\"Calculation address must start with 'fn.'\")` (core.py:33-34). The one addressing invariant PCR enforces is single-writer-per-address plus direct-result rewrite, not a naming rule: `prior = self._writers.get(output.address); if prior is not None: raise ValueError(f\"PCR '{self.name}' has multiple writers for '{output.address}'\")` (pcr.py:120-122), exercised by test_first_class.py:38-49 and, in the declarative (non-executing) authoring surface, by graph.py:73-74 and test_graph.py:31-36. Observed address shapes in production S1 (S1.mmd:1-33, PrincipleComponentRender.yaml:1-60) are hierarchical dot-paths that encode stage/subsystem/artifact, e.g. `px.course.canonicalPixels`, `px.s1.exp.maskComponents.part.blackMask`, `px.badges.objects`, `px.badges.px`, `px.badges.muted`, `fn.s1.exp.maskComponents.selectHsvMask`, `fn.s1.exp.badgeAssembly.assemble` \u2014 the \"root\" segment (`px`, `fn`, `scratch`) is a bare type tag, not a discriminating first segment; discrimination happens in the later segments. `scratch.s1.ownershipSummary` (s1.py:36) is quick_anno's own convention for Python-derived, non-production Parts, kept syntactically distinct from `px.*` production Parts but with no code that treats `scratch.` specially. graph.py's own docstring states the ownership split this addressing serves: \"This deliberately does not execute ChainSpot calculations. It composes the same small graph shape used by the S0/S1 Mermaid proof and can emit PCR JSON plus a Mermaid view. Execution remains owned by ChainSpot/PxC.\" (graph.py:32-34), echoed in README.md:8: \"This is intentionally not a second ChainSpot executor. Python authors a graph; ChainSpot/PxC remains the intended execution owner.\" So addressing here is about authorship/execution ownership, not about connection or discrimination value per segment \u2014 the owner's log(N) framing is not yet a rule anywhere in this tip.",
+  "viewer_and_pcr_render": "Two separate things stand in for \"the viewer\" and neither is a Tick-keyed rendered PCR/Kibana surface yet (the ELK/\"PCRs are Kibana\" phrasing itself postdates this commit by two days and is absent here \u2014 recording as missing). (1) `PCR.mermaid()` (pcr.py:179-218) is a pure structural view: one node per distinct Part address, one subgraph per Tick containing its Calculation ids, edges labeled by the bound argument name, with no values and no reads/writes beyond the graph shape itself \u2014 e.g. it emits `subgraph Tick_Tick1[\"Tick: Tick1\"]` and `p0 -->|source| example` (verified against test_graph.py:42-46). It is written to a file (`python-S1.mmd`, s1.py:73) but nothing renders it \u2014 README.md:43 states the proof \"does not yet ... emit existing PCR YAML byte-for-byte, or plug the generated Mermaid into a browser renderer.\" (2) The actual thing a person visually inspects is `materialize_s1_neon.py`'s 7-panel neon contact sheet (badge boxes, production masks, owned px, added-mute px, total muted, removed px, remaining-over-checkerboard; materialize_s1_neon.py:132-140), which is keyed by the whole S1 Stage snapshot, not by the six Ticks the same computation is decomposed into in S1.mmd (RasterInput, BlackMask, WhiteMask, BadgeAssembly, WhiteDigitRecognition, BadgeOutputs \u2014 S1.mmd:6-33): the materializer only ever touches `snapshot['ownedPixels']`/`mutedPixels`/`badges'` as flat, pre-aggregated sets (materialize_s1_neon.py:118-130), so no panel can be attributed to one Tick. S1_CHECKPOINT.md:101 states the acceptance rule for what a person sees: \"A subtle render is not accepted as the first correctness proof; **neon first** is the investigation convention.\" Separately, production ChainSpot's own inspection surface is named directly as unfinished in review/s1-ownership/README.md:36-38: \"Still pending before landing: downstream contract-consumer compatibility, browser PartInspector numbers/ownership display, and removal of positional final-output assumptions in scripts/inspect-s1-parts.mjs.\" \u2014 i.e. even the production \"PartInspector\" browser viewer this experiment feeds does not yet show S1's numbers correctly.",
+  "storage": "No DuckDB and no storage backend of any kind exist in this tip \u2014 a repo-wide `grep -ril duckdb` under the extracted tree returned zero matches. PxC is a bare in-process Python dict, nothing more: `self._values: dict[str, Any] = {}` plus `self._calculations: dict[str, Calculation[Any, Any]] = {}` (core.py:50-51), with `set`/`get`/`has` operating directly on that dict (core.py:53-66) and no persistence, no content addressing, and no serialization method on PxC itself. There is no \"kind\" or dtype tag on a Part or a stored value (core.py's `_values: dict[str, Any]`), so nothing here differentiates a raster Part from a scalar Part at the storage layer \u2014 this matches the exact gap pyto's tick-observability-ledger.md section 3 item 4 already names as missing in pyto's own core.py. What is archived, and why: `export_s1_snapshot.cjs` runs the real TS S0+S1 stage contracts once and freezes the result to disk as `snapshot.json` (schema-tagged: `schema: 'chainspot-quick-anno-s1-snapshot@1'`, export_s1_snapshot.cjs:48) containing `panels` (PNG filenames), `s0Receipt`/`s1Receipt` text, `counts`, per-badge `bbox`/`confidence`/`whitePx`/`blackPx`/`ownedPx`/`mutedPx`, and flat `ownedPixels`/`mutedPixels` index arrays (export_s1_snapshot.cjs:47-78) plus `s0.receipt.txt`/`s1.receipt.txt` (export_s1_snapshot.cjs:81-82). The purpose stated in S1_CHECKPOINT.md:105-107 is a deliberate boundary, not a caching decision: \"Today the production runtime executes S1; Python consumes its resulting testimony/materials and performs experimental calculations over them. That boundary is deliberate: first make PxC/PQL/PCR behavior pleasant in Python, then promote only proven calculations.\" So the archival is a one-shot snapshot-for-transfer (JS runtime -> JSON file -> Python PxC), not a content-addressed cache with hit/miss semantics \u2014 there is no revision key, no digest, and nothing here answers the open pyto `{?} CacheInvalidation` or `{?} StorageKinds` questions.",
+  "materialization_and_ticks": "`PCR.run(pxc)` (pcr.py:135-177) executes every Tick's Calculations in declaration order, resolves each binding to either a stored Part (`px:<address>`) or a same-run result (`fn:<calculation_id>`), calls the Calculation via `pxc.call`, writes the result into PxC when an `into` Part is declared, and accumulates a `CalculationTestimony(id, calculation, inputs, args, into)` per invocation grouped into a `TickTestimony(name, calculations)` per Tick, returned as `PcrRun(pcr, ticks, results)` (pcr.py:59-77, 135-177) \u2014 structurally identical to what tick-observability-ledger.md documents for pyto's own `pcr.py`, confirming this ChainSpot file is that lineage's direct source. Values ARE live in memory (`results: dict[str, Any]` on PcrRun, pcr.py:78, populated at pcr.py:162) but nothing here serializes them \u2014 `s1.py:72` writes only the single terminal `scratch.s1.ownershipSummary` value to `python-summary.json`, not `run.results` or any per-Tick file, so per-Tick materialization of intermediate values does not exist in this tip either, matching the exact gap pyto's own ledger names. Tick granularity is real in the composition graph \u2014 S1.mmd:6-33 shows 6 named Ticks (RasterInput, BlackMask, WhiteMask, BadgeAssembly, WhiteDigitRecognition, BadgeOutputs) each with multiple Calculations \u2014 but the neon correctness materialization collapses all of that to Stage granularity: `materialize_s1_neon.py` never reads a Tick name or Calculation id, only the flat `ownedPixels`/`mutedPixels`/`badges` fields of `snapshot.json` (materialize_s1_neon.py:113-130). The delegate checklist that this checkpoint proposes as the reusable procedure for extending to S2+ is explicit and ordered (S1_CHECKPOINT.md:109-122): \"1. take the prior Stage's PxC material as input; 2. expose the smallest real runtime snapshot needed for the investigation; 3. seed first-class Python Parts; 4. express new experimental work as named Calculations in a PCR; 5. publish useful scratch Parts into Python PxC; 6. query with PQL rather than parallel ad-hoc state; 7. materialize a **neon-first** visual correctness check; 8. visually inspect the materialization before claiming success; 9. emit Mermaid/PCR testimony from the same composition; 10. add a convenience only after real repeated friction demonstrates it.\"",
+  "receipts_and_telemetry": "The Python-side testimony shape carries no value, no digest, and no timing at all: `CalculationTestimony` is exactly `id: str`, `calculation: str`, `inputs: dict[str, str]`, `args: dict[str, Any]`, `into: str | None` (pcr.py:59-65) \u2014 inputs are refs (`px:...`/`fn:...`, pcr.py:149,157), never values, and there is no `Observation`/digest/duration type anywhere in this package (that concept appears only later, in pyto proper, per tick-observability-ledger.md). This is the \"dumb receipt\" pattern origin.md complains about, still present at this checkpoint on the ChainSpot side. Real production telemetry, by contrast, is ad hoc and domain-shaped rather than PxC/Tick-shaped: `review/s1-ownership/receipt.json` records `\"rule\": \"outer-border-bbox\"`, `\"reviewMarginPx\": 8`, `\"partition\": \"every raster pixel exactly once\"`, `\"executionMs\": 912.8246479999998`, `\"owned\": 37295`, `\"muted\": 3949`, `\"remaining\": 2645826`, `\"total\": 2687070`, and a `perBadge` array of `{id, number, bbox, components, unaccountedButOwned, excluded}` (receipt.json:1-20) \u2014 a single wall-clock number for the whole Stage, no per-Tick or per-Calculation timing, no writer address, and no reference to a PxC address at all; it is a human-audit accounting document, not a replayable trace. `s0.receipt.txt`/`s1.receipt.txt` (export_s1_snapshot.cjs:81-82) are plain text, produced by the TS stage contracts' own `receiptText`, and are opaque strings from Python's point of view \u2014 quick_anno never parses them, only archives them.",
