+  "addressing": "Root/prefix rule, verbatim: `f3ecab3:packages/quick_anno_py/chainspot_quick_anno/core.py:33-34` \u2014 `Calculation.__post_init__`: `if not self.address.startswith(\"fn.\"):  raise ValueError(\"Calculation address must start with 'fn.'\")`. Part addresses carry no such enforced prefix in Python but every example and test uses `px.` (`core.py:11-13`: \"A semantic PxC address. Identity is the address, not the current value.\"; e.g. `f3ecab3:packages/quick_anno_py/tests/test_first_class.py:6-7` `Part(\"px.badges\")`, `Part(\"px.remaining.afterBadges\")`). The TS reference this transfers from enforces the same two-namespace rule structurally, not just by convention: `f3ecab3:packages/alg/src/stages/S0/exp/mermaid-pcr/compiler.ts:31-32` \u2014 a Mermaid node \"must label fn.* or px.*\" or compilation fails (`fail(\\`node '${node[1]}' must label fn.* or px.*.\\`)`), and `compiler.ts:73` rejects \"PxC-to-PxC edges require a Calculation\" \u2014 i.e. a Part can never address another Part directly; every edge into a Part must pass through a named `fn.` Calculation. This is the closest thing in the tip to an explicit addressing rule, and it is exactly two segments deep (kind prefix, then a dotted semantic path) \u2014 there is no root segment beyond the `px`/`fn` kind tag itself, so the tip does not engage the owner's \"meaningless root\" critique at all; the root here is a bare kind discriminator (`px`/`fn`), not a connective name. No mount, namespace-registration, or per-application addressing concept appears anywhere in the tip: `grep -rn \"mount\"` across the extracted tip found only 2 unrelated hits (LAB page/pin \\\"namespaces\\\" in `scripts/chainspot-lab/README.md:185`, a pre-existing doc unrelated to this commit) and no `mount`/registration logic in any of the new PxC/PCR/PQL files. Alignment between the graph-authoring layer and the executable layer is done by direct-result rewrite, not addressing: once a Part has a writer inside the same graph, an