+  "storage": "No storage backend, DuckDB, or content-addressing mechanism is introduced or discussed anywhere in this tip. `grep -ri duckdb` across the entire extracted tip returned zero hits. The Python `PxC` (`f3ecab3:packages/quick_anno_py/chainspot_quick_anno/core.py:46-93`) is an in-memory `dict[str, Any]` only \u2014 no serialization, no persistence, no revision/digest concept: `set()` just records `\\\"replacement\\\"` vs `\\\"new-address\\\"` write kind (`core.py:62-66`) and there is no `sha256`/digest field anywhere in the Python package. The TS `PCR` does carry a content-identity idea, but it is a run-result hash over already-produced testimony, not a storage backend: `f3ecab3:packages/alg/src/exec/pcr.ts:69-76` computes `runResultId = sha256HexSyncText(canonicalJson({planFingerprint, paramsHash, ticks: ...frozenTickIdentity}))`, and is explicit about what it does not cover: `pcr.ts:29-31,84-85` \u2014 \\\"Explicitly bounded: opaque PxC values are not silently claimed as content-frozen\\\" / `runResultIdentityLimitation: 'opaque PxC output values require an emitted Materialization'\\\". \\\"What was archived and why\\\" \u2014 nothing was archived in this tip; the only archival-adjacent note is `f3ecab3:packages/alg/src/stages/S0/exp/mermaid-pcr/README.md:23-24`: \\\"The combined S0-S1 graph and S0-S1.pcr.yaml are historical drafts, not inputs to this entry point,\\\" i.e. earlier PCR YAML kept as history but explicitly not live material. Missing entirely from this tip: any statement on per-namespace storage backend choice, S3-opposite specialized-backend design, or warm-start budget \u2014 those all belong to the pyto research docs (`ULTRACODE-WEEK.md` Reframings 2 and 5), not to this ChainSpot commit.",
+  "materialization_and_ticks": "Tick shape matches pyto's `Tick -> Calculation[]` exactly and is the direct transfer source: Python `Tick` is `name` + ordered `calculations: list[Invocation]` (`f3ecab3:packages/quick_anno_py/chainspot_quick_anno/pcr.py:28-56`); `PCR.run()` iterates Ticks in declared order, and for each Tick emits one `TickTestimony(name, calculations)` (`pcr.py:135-177`), so a Tick is literally the unit at which \\\"its Calculations become observable\\\" (the origin.md phrase) \u2014 `PcrRun.ticks` is a tuple of these, returned alongside `results: dict[str, Any]` holding every invocation's actual value (`pcr.py:74-78,177`). This is a materially richer Tick than TS's: the Python `PcrRun.results` holds live values keyed by invocation id (`pcr.py:161-162,177`), whereas the TS `TickTestimony`/`Pcr` never carries values, only digests and a `runResultId` \u2014 consistent with the tick-observability-ledger's finding (`tick-observability-ledger.md:66`) that \\\"the actual computed Part value ... is only ever digested on the way to a file,\\\" and this ChainSpot-side tip does not close that gap either: no materializer function exists in it, only the authoring/testimony layer. Declaration-order execution and the writer-uniqueness invariant are enforced identically on both sides: Python `PCR.calc` raises \\\"multiple writers\\\" (`pcr.py:118-123`) and duplicate-id (`pcr.py:109-110`); TS compiler enforces the same plus forward-dependency/cycle rejection (`compiler.ts:69-80,93`, \\\"forward dependency or cycle\\\"), and TS execution is declaration-order per `f3ecab3:packages/alg/src/stages/S0/exp/mermaid-pcr/README.md:26-27` (\\\"declaration-order execution ... direct function-result arrows\\\"). PCR-as-non-engine is stated three times independently in this tip, at increasing levels of force: Python README (\\\"This is intentionally not a second ChainSpot executor... ChainSpot/PxC remains the intended execution owner,\\\" `experiments/quick-anno-python/README.