+  "addressing": "DECIDED: the mount root is deliberately kept OUTSIDE the PxC semantic address space rather than becoming a namespace prefix inside it. mounts.ts:3-7: \"A root mounted above an ordinary PxC. The root is intentionally external to PxC's semantic address space: values remain px.* and calculations remain fn.* inside every mounted world.\" The root id is \"currently ImgID\" (mounts.ts:11), i.e. one root per course/image, and `get()`/`mount()` do no rewriting: mounts.ts:15 \"Return the exact PxC mounted at root; no address rewriting occurs.\" The test that pins this down is named for exactly the owner's connection concern \u2014 pxcRootMounts.test.ts:6 \"keeps ImgID outside the PxC address space\" \u2014 and asserts the negative directly: pxcRootMounts.test.ts:16-17 `expect(dash.has('px.DashsTrack.s1.badges')).toBe(false)` / `expect(north.has('px.NorthPark.s1.badges')).toBe(false)`, i.e. the root name is explicitly never spliced into an address. Connection is instead achieved by identity repetition, not hierarchy: the same flat address (e.g. `px.s1.badges` or `px.course.canonicalPixels`) is independently resolvable inside every mounted root, which is what the batch warmer calls out as the point: warm-dev-pxc-roots.mjs:60 \"// Wide access proof: one semantic address, independently resolved in every mounted world.\" and its emitted report literally states the rule: warm-dev-pxc-roots.mjs:69 `rootSemantics: '<ImgID>/{px.*,fn.*}'`, `addressMutation: false`. `roots()`/`entries()`/`slice()` (mounts.ts:17-22) give stable insertion-order, cheap grouped projection across roots without cloning: mounts.ts:21 \"Make a cheap root-level slice; mounted PxCs themselves are shared.\" This is a different answer than a namespace-prefixed address (`px.<root>.s1.badges`), and it is a live counterpoint to pyto's open `{?} StorageKinds` framing of \"backend chosen by address prefix\" (questions.md:179-184) \u2014 here the chosen mechanism is an external mount registry keyed by a meaningful id (the image/course identity) with the address space itself staying uniform and unrewritten across mounts, so \"connection\" comes from address-identity reuse across worlds rather than from a root segment carrying discriminating meaning inside each address. Adjacent but separate: the LAB CLI's \"Search Pages\" are explicitly called out the same way \u2014 named overlay namespaces over one shared raster, not copies: `.task/LAB-SCOPE-V0.md:102` \"Search Pages are named overlay namespaces on the same canonical raster, allowing scratch/notes/final surfaces without duplicating image data,\" and `scripts/chainspot-lab/README.md:185` \"Pages are visibility/mutation namespaces, not raster copies.\" No explicit rule is stated anywhere in this tip for how a root id must be chosen or must discriminate beyond \"non-empty\" (mounts.ts:30 `if (!root) throw new Error('PxC mounts: root id must be non-empty.')`) and \"one PxC per root, no silent remount\" (mounts.ts:31-34 throws if a different PxC is remounted at the same root) \u2014 there is no stated rule that a root must itself carry semantic connection the way the owner's \"wasted log(N) potential\" statement asks; it is treated as an opaque, already-meaningful key (an image id) rather than a designed address segment.",
+  "viewer_and_pcr_render": "Per-Tick, and it is explicitly execution-free: pcr.ts:17-21 \"Storybook-facing composition of already-executed Tick testimony. PCR has deliberately no run() method: the production gateway is the only execution authority and hands this function the receipts it already produced.\" `composePcr()` (pcr.ts:51-88) takes a `PcrSpec{id,title,tickIds}` plus the plan and the already-produced `TickTestimony[]`, and only assembles `{operation, testimony}` pairs per tick \u2014 it throws if a listed Tick \"never ran\" (pcr.ts:66, exercised by pcr.test.ts:135-139) or is named twice (pcr.ts:61). Identity of the whole PCR is a sha256 over plan fingerprint + params hash + a frozen projection of each tick's testimony (pcr.ts:70-76, `frozenTickIdentity` at pcr.ts:35-49), explicitly bounded: pcr.ts:29-31 \"Explicitly bounded: opaque PxC values are not silently claimed as content-frozen\" / `runResultIdentityLimitation: 'opaque PxC output values require an emitted Materialization'`. What a person sees is per-Tick structure, not raw values by default: the viewer route `/lab/pcr` (a Storybook-reused molecule) opens on a checked default plan \u2014 pcrInspectionPlan.ts:15-23 `DEFAULT_PCR_INSPECTION_PLAN = {pcrId:'badge-pcr', tickId:'badgeStage.masks', specimenId:'badge-0', materializationView:'composed', zoom:6, showGrid:false, showReceipt:true}` \u2014 and a set of named `MaterializationView`s the user can flip between: pcrInspectionPlan.ts:1-12 `PCR_MATERIALIZATION_VIEWS = ['raw','bw','ownership','residue-after','composed','components','available','explained','unexplained','relationships']`. Loading the default plan is fail-loud, not silently degraded: pcrInspectionPlan.ts:25-31 \"Fail the staging build instead of publishing a blank or drifted default plan,\" checked against materialized PCR/Tick/specimen identity actually present. The render is fed by `library.pcrs`/`library.specimens` from a server-side `materializeBadgeSpecimens()` load function (+page.server.ts:1-8), then `PcrInspectionHost.svelte` selects the named PCR and Tick (`library.pcrs.find(...)`, PcrInspectionHost.svelte:20,30) and renders a `TickInspection` panel (testimony) beside a separate, clearly labeled \"Projection-only MaterializationView\" panel whose header states the boundary explicitly: PcrInspectionHost.svelte:90-92 \"Projection-only MaterializationView\" / \"View Args never cross the production gateway\". Changing the materialization view (zoom, grid, which projection) only changes a separate render-identity hash, never the run identity: pcr.ts:90-93 \"View identity is a pure projection of a frozen PCR; it cannot execute CV,\" proven by pcr.test.ts:63-72 (`pcrRenderId` differs across `{zoom:2}` vs `{zoom:4}` while `pcr.runResultId` stays identical). The whole staging surface's purpose is stated directly: src/lib/evidence-workbench/README.md:27-30 \"`/lab/pcr` reuses the Storybook inspection molecules against the same materialized computation. It opens with the checked `DEFAULT_PCR_INSPECTION_PLAN`... The staging build fails loudly if the corpus is absent or any default PCR, Tick, or specimen identity has drifted.\" Note a second, unrelated sense of \"PCR\" appears in this branch: docs/handoffs/BADGE-PCR-DISCOVERY-SCRIPT.md:1-25 uses \"PCR\" to mean the git-commit narrative/history structure of the badge investigation (\"the useful PCR is the one so faithful to the actual algorithm that the missing counters become obvious accidentally,\" line 25), not the `Pcr` render object \u2014 the two usages should not be conflated when porting vocabulary.",
+  "storage": "No DuckDB, no external database, and no disk-backed content-addressed store appear anywhere in this tip's diff (grep for \"duckdb\" across the extracted tree returned nothing) \u2014 storage stays in-memory. `PxCRootMounts` (mounts.ts:25-53) is a plain `Map<PxCRootId, PxC>`; mounting is \"one already-materialized world\" (mounts.ts:11), i.e. the store holds whole `PxC` objects (themselves `Map`-backed `ExecBoard`s, board.ts:59-79), not individual serialized rows. `PxC` itself (board.ts:38-45) is described as \"PxC's browser-safe address space,\" with `fork()` explicitly a snapshot (\"Snapshot addresses and registered functions; immutable Part values remain shared,\" board.ts:41) \u2014 copy-on-fork, not persistence. What is archived is receipts and content-addressed artifact references, not raw Parts: `ArtifactRef` (contract.ts:97-105) is \"Content-addressed reference to an artifact the sink has stored... `uri` is sink-defined (a file:// path from the Node sink, an in-memory handle from a browser/collector sink) \u2014 the exec core never interprets it, only carries it through the receipt,\" and the contract file states the boundary up front: contract.ts:6-9 \"R1 (browser-safe core): everything below describes DATA. Writing an artifact or receipt to disk is the job of an INJECTED sink the caller hands to executeCompiledPlan \u2014 never something a type here performs.\" So \"storage\" in this tip is a pluggable sink for artifact bytes (Node vs browser/in-memory) plus an in-process root-mount registry for whole PxC worlds; there is no backend-per-kind facade (raster/graph/JSON) as pyto's `{?} StorageKinds` proposes, and no persistence across process restarts is implemented \u2014 `warm-dev-pxc-roots.mjs` rebuilds every course's PxC from the corpus each run (lines 32-58) rather than reading a persisted store. One retained-caching convention does appear at the PCR-spec level: S0.pcr.yaml:15-16 declares `last: cache: FullImage`, i.e. the yaml names which upstream value the last Tick should re-cache \u2014 but no code in this tip's tree consumes `S0.pcr.yaml` (grep for the filename outside itself found no reader), so this is a documented/aspirational schema, not a wired mechanism.",
+  "materialization_and_ticks": "The Tick/testimony shape is materialized as `Receipt`, and `TickTestimony` is a direct type alias of it: contract.ts:143-147 \"A Tick is the existing gateway Receipt understood as inspection testimony: exact addresses in, frozen calculations, exact addresses out. It adds no execution authority and deliberately has no run() method.\" / `export type TickTestimony = Receipt;`. A `Receipt` (contract.ts:118-141) carries `opId`, `frozenCalculations` (each `FrozenCalculation`: `address`, `implementationHash`, and an honestly-scoped `identityScope:'runtime-function-body'` with `limitation:'called helpers, constants, templates, and assets are not covered'`, contract.ts:65-68 \u2014 the same scope-limitation language pyto's `implementation_sha256` carries), `startedAtMs`/`durationMs`, `declaredConsumes`/`declaredProduces` vs `actualConsumes`/`actualProduces`, `writes: PxWriteTestimony[]` classified as `'new-address'|'refinement'|'replacement'` (contract.ts:73-76, exercised in pxcTick.test.ts:38-54), `probes: Probe[]` (named numeric observations, contract.ts:108-110), and `artifacts: ArtifactRef[]`. Access is tracked live against the board (`trackAccess`, exercised in pxcTick.test.ts): a missing read throws by Tick name \u2014 pxcTick.test.ts:33-35 `\"PxC: Tick 'test.double' read missing address 'px.test.missing'.\"` \u2014 and an opt-in `accessConformance:'exact'` mode refuses undeclared traffic instead of recording a plausible receipt: pxcTick.test.ts:114-139, throwing `/extra reads=\\[px\\.test\\.hiddenInput\\]/`, and separately refuses a calculation bound under the wrong `fn.*` address (pxcTick.test.ts:94-112, `/calculation bindings disagree/`). This is a concrete, already-shipped implementation of pyto's open `{?} SilentRules` question (should pcr.py raise at declaration/access instead of failing only at run) \u2014 here JS already fails loud on exactly that shadow case. A full multi-Tick chain is demonstrated end to end in s0IntakePcr.test.ts:45-49: `run.pcr.ticks.map(t=>t.operation.id)` equals `['source.decodeFullImage','source.cropUDiscChrome','source.cacheFullImage']`, i.e. three real Ticks (decode, crop, cache) each producing its own testimony, composed into one `Pcr`. `PCR.mermaid()`-equivalent visibility is not present in this tip (no mermaid export found in the diffed files); the render is purely the Storybook/staging JSON+HTML view described above. The batch warmer (`warm-dev-pxc-roots.mjs`) is the closest thing to a materialization-observability harness for the mounts themselves: it times mounting every dev-corpus course's post-S1 world against `TARGET_MS = 40_000` / `HARD_MS = 55_000` (lines 9-10) \u2014 a much looser cold-start budget than pyto's stated 5000 ms CV target (questions.md WarmupBudget), worth flagging as a discrepancy if this convention is ported.",
+  "receipts_and_telemetry": "The `Receipt`/`TickTestimony` shape (contract.ts:118-141, quoted above) is the telemetry unit: per-invocation `durationMs`/`startedAtMs`, declared-vs-actual consumes/produces, typed writes, named numeric `Probe`s, and content-addressed `ArtifactRef`s. `ArtifactRef` (contract.ts:97-105) is `{id, kind, sha256, uri, dims?}` where `kind: ArtifactKind` is a closed enum \u2014 contract.ts:78-89: `'rgba'|'mask'|'scalarField'|'orientationField'|'componentSet'|'candidateSet'|'badgeEvidence'|'m1Representation'|'m2Representation'|'polyline'|'measurementTable'` \u2014 and `dims?: RasterDims{width,height}` is present only for raster kinds \"whose extractor knew the shape,\" with an explicit non-inference rule: contract.ts:93-96 \"A consumer receiving undefined MUST NOT infer dimensions from byte length -- decline to rasterize instead.\" This `ArtifactKind`+`dims` pair is a direct, already-built answer to the gap pyto's tick-observability-ledger.md section 3 item 4 names as missing (\"No way to tell an image Part from a scalar Part... no kind, dtype, or renderer tag\"). At the mount/warm level, telemetry is a flat JSON report: warm-dev-pxc-roots.mjs:68-78 `{rootSemantics, addressMutation, mountedRoots, targetMs, hardMs, elapsedMs, withinTarget, rows:[{label,root,input,croppedPx}], wideCanonicalPixels:[{root,label,widthPx,heightPx}]}`, written to disk and uploaded as a CI artifact by `.github/workflows/pxc-root-warm.yml:29-37`. `Pcr` itself carries a schema tag for future evolution \u2014 pcr.ts:23 `readonly schema: 'chainspot-pcr@1'` \u2014 a versioned-schema convention pyto's `testimony.json`/`retained.json` do not currently carry.",
