+  "addressing": "This tip never uses PxC's Tick/Calculation address grammar as pyto defines it (`fn.*`, `px.*` per pyto's own docs), but the S1 experiment tree (packages/alg/src/stages/S1/exp/badge-assembly/REVIEW.md) shows a real, running instance of address-as-storage-key that is directly comparable: \"The caller receives results and the input PxC stores them at px.pql.<Stage>.results.\" (chainspot:packages/alg/src/stages/S1/exp/badge-assembly/REVIEW.md:12) and feature-discovery's dependency declaration reads addresses as preconditions: \"Requires warm px.s1.exp.badgeAssembly.badgeCandidates and px.s1.discovery.labels.\" (chainspot:packages/alg/src/stages/S1/exp/feature-discovery/REVIEW.md:4). experiments/parts-inspector/README.md shows a third namespace, `px.view.*`, reserved for UI-only state that never triggers computation: \"Selection, visibility, isolation, and framing use `px.view.partsInspector`; those controls do not invoke Calculations.\" (chainspot:experiments/parts-inspector/README.md:3-5). So the *root* segments actually in use are `px.pql.<Stage>.results`, `px.s1.<...>`, `px.view.<...>` \u2014 each root names a distinct kind of thing (a Stage's declared outputs, a specific exp module's Parts, UI-only view state), which is a working example of the owner's \"the root must carry connection, not a generic label\" rule, though none of these docs states that rule explicitly \u2014 it is inferred from the naming, not asserted.\n\nThe clearest explicit addressing statement in the tip is about content addressing, not namespace roots: .task/CHSPT-82.md specifies a canonical composite's identity as a hash over its bytes, not a hand-assigned label: \"canonical composite materialization: real pixel flattening (Node-runnable, no canvas) + content-addressed `imageId` = sha256 over `[u32be widthPx][u32be heightPx][raw RGBA bytes]`, documented and pinned by a known-tiny-composite unit test.\" (chainspot:.task/CHSPT-82.md:270-271). That is a genuine \"address carries connection\" instance \u2014 the address IS the content, so two identical composites from different pipelines collide on purpose and become the same document \u2014 directly resonant with Reframing 4's \"content addressing as the document identity\" for PxC (pyto/research/ULTRACODE-WEEK.md:83).\n\nSearch Pages give a second, non-computational namespace convention worth reading against pyto's mounts: \"Search Pages are named overlay namespaces on the same canonical raster, allowing scratch/notes/final surfaces without duplicating image data.\" (chainspot:.task/LAB-SCOPE-V0.md:102) and \"Pages are visibility/mutation namespaces, not raster copies.\" (chainspot:scripts/chainspot-lab/README.md:185). No Page name is privileged: \"Page names and retained/promoted semantics remain user-controlled and dogfoodable; no specific Page name is privileged in the state model.\" (chainspot:.task/LAB-SCOPE-V0.md:104) \u2014 an anti-generic-root discipline in spirit, applied to a different address space (raster overlays, not PxC Parts).\n\nThe matrix-review experiment states an explicit PxC addressing formula, closest of anything in this tip to a \"how addresses connect\" rule: \"LAB stores actual calibration and pose profiles at PxC addresses keyed by source bytes, frame, seed, masks, sensor parameters and calculation revision.\" (chainspot:experiments/dashs-track-edge-sensing/matrix-review/README.md:23) \u2014 i.e. the address key is a tuple of everything that could change the value's meaning, so that \"identical profiles can feed different predicates without counting as independent evidence\" (chainspot:experiments/dashs-track-edge-sensing/LAB-BROAD-TESTING-PROPOSAL.md:57-58). That is a concrete, load-bearing definition of what a discriminating address segment must include \u2014 directly transferable to pyto's own address design. No explicit \"root must not be generic\" rule is ever written down in this tip; that owner statement postdates it and is not itself present in the ChainSpot repo. Missing: any mount/prefix-to-backend routing table, any registration step for a namespace, any statement that addressing is unregistered by design (pyto's own \"no registration\" framing is absent here \u2014 must be recorded as missing per instructions).",
+  "viewer_and_pcr_render": "badge-assembly/REVIEW.md documents a real, working PCR: \"PrincipleComponentRender.yaml owns the full experimental S1 order and arguments. The Stage factory prepares inputs/registers implementations and delegates to the shared runner.\" (chainspot:packages/alg/src/stages/S1/exp/badge-assembly/REVIEW.md:7) \u2014 confirming pyto's PrincipleComponentRender naming is not a pyto invention but a live ChainSpot artifact (a YAML file). Its Ticks/forks are PxC forks, not named Tick objects: \"A PxC fork snapshots slot and function maps. It shares immutable values, including raster buffers. Each run's writes stay in its own fork. A following Stage can consume the chosen result's pxc.\" (chainspot:packages/alg/src/stages/S1/exp/badge-assembly/REVIEW.md:11). Per-invocation testimony is retained but not yet joined into a per-Tick view, exactly the gap tick-observability-ledger.md section 3 names for pyto: \"Runs retain declared call, actualCall, effective args, inputs, and outputs for differentiation. This is recorded material, not a comparison score.\" (chainspot:packages/alg/src/stages/S1/exp/badge-assembly/REVIEW.md:12).\n\nWhat a person actually sees is described concretely: \"The shared browser-safe JS renderer produces SVG from exact pixel membership. The HTML inspector reads Calculation outputs, lets the user select an item and toggle its component Parts, and exports the same SVG projection. View controls perform no CV calculations.\" (chainspot:packages/alg/src/stages/S1/exp/badge-assembly/REVIEW.md:14) \u2014 pixels/masks rendered as colored SVG regions, selectable per candidate, Parts toggle-able, and the viewer is read-only (never computes). experiments/parts-inspector/README.md gives the fed values precisely: \"`receipt.json` records the actual Default run: 3 Ticks, 9 Calculations, 952 black components, 176 white components, 18 candidates, no incomplete assemblies. `candidate.svg` shows the first candidate with a possible loop; `candidate.png` is that SVG rasterized at 768 density. Amber is plate material, cyan border, white digit material, pink possible loop, and gray unselected pixels.\" (chainspot:experiments/parts-inspector/README.md:18-22). This is a Tick-count-labeled render fed by a receipt.json and a PxC seeded with saved raster bytes: \"The staging `/parts` page seeds PxC with exact saved S0 raster bytes and runs the existing S1 YAML once.\" (chainspot:experiments/parts-inspector/README.md:3-4). A UI-decision worth porting: \"Sam's resolved preference: selecting another result starts with every Part visible, including Parts shared with a previously viewed result.\" (chainspot:experiments/parts-inspector/README.md:31-32) \u2014 a default-visible-until-hidden rule for a multi-candidate viewer.\n\nThe tip also records, via docs/unported/engine-dev72-lineage.md, a full post-mortem of an EARLIER browser receipt viewer (T5) that was built and then explicitly rejected for pyto/ChessLab-style porting: \"T5's receipt viewer disagrees with T6's receipt shape, and nothing catches it... The viewer survives only because it renders `work` as `<pre>{json(...)}</pre>`\" (chainspot:docs/unported/engine-dev72-lineage.md:339-345), and the verdict: \"T5's receipt viewer (677 lines of Svelte) \u2014 its data model is wrong against the shipped receipt. Rebuild from the real `Receipt` type.\" (chainspot:docs/unported/engine-dev72-lineage.md:556-558) and the regeneration note: \"Reconcile the receipt `work` shape before writing a viewer. One definition, one fixture, generated from a real sink run rather than hand-authored.\" (chainspot:docs/unported/engine-dev72-lineage.md:532-534). This is a direct, concrete lesson for pyto's Day-3 tick-viewer: freeze the receipt shape from a real run before writing the viewer against it, never hand-author the fixture. Missing: no per-Stage (vs per-Tick) toggle is described anywhere in this tip; nothing states what \"per Stage\" would show differently from \"per Tick.\"",
+  "storage": "The tip's dominant storage decision is documented as a deliberate archival act, not a backend choice: docs/unported/README.md explains the whole 11-file docs/unported/** addition as \"we made a deliberate choice to **abandon the code and keep the knowledge**. Ten extractors read the old branches read-only and wrote down, in prose, what each piece of work detects, why it exists, what pixels it looks at, every magic number it depends on, and what evidence (if any) proves it works... The old repo stays read-only forever; when this machine is reset, these files are what survives.\" (chainspot:docs/unported/README.md:6). This is the ChainSpot analogue of pyto's \"archived because it was too slow\" pattern ({?} WarmupBudget, DuckDB) \u2014 here the archival trigger is an irreconcilable directory-layout fork, not a latency budget, but the pattern (keep the knowledge, discard the code, mark verdicts explicitly) is directly reusable. Concretely one branch is marked for deletion with justification: \"`lab/dev72-algorithm` should simply be deleted \u2014 it contains no file and no hunk that `engine/dev72` does not already have.\" (chainspot:docs/unported/engine-dev72-lineage.md:570-571), and another spec is marked discard: \"[`lab-render-evidence.md`] ... **discard** ... Strict subset of `engine/dev72`\" (chainspot:docs/unported/README.md:14).\n\nContent addressing appears once, precisely: a canonical pixel composite's identity is its own hash, per .task/CHSPT-82.md: \"canonical composite materialization: real pixel flattening (Node-runnable, no canvas) + content-addressed `imageId` = sha256 over `[u32be widthPx][u32be heightPx][raw RGBA bytes]`, documented and pinned by a known-tiny-composite unit test.\" (chainspot:.task/CHSPT-82.md:270-271). No DuckDB, no named alternative backend, and no \"cold-start budget\" language of any kind appears anywhere in this tip's added or changed markdown (grepped `docs/`, `experiments/`, `scripts/chainspot-lab/`, `.task/`, `packages/**/README*` for \"duckdb\" with zero hits) \u2014 recording as missing, matching pyto's own {?} WarmupBudget framing that DuckDB was tried and archived elsewhere, not here.\n\nThe RATCHETS.md law encodes a storage/measurement-trust discipline that touches receipts directly: \"**STEP 0, before anything else: verify the measurement itself**, per the `receipt-reconcile` skill, BEFORE treating a broken threshold as a footgun firing... A case whose measurement fails either check is a **MEASUREMENT DEFECT**: fix the measuring, leave the threshold untouched.\" (chainspot:docs/RATCHETS.md:9-16) \u2014 a rule that receipts (stored evidence) outrank the reader's threshold assumptions, reusable as a governance pattern for pyto's own retained records.",
+  "materialization_and_ticks": "The strongest direct hit is the parts-inspector run summary itself acting as a materialization report: \"3 Ticks, 9 Calculations, 952 black components, 176 white components, 18 candidates, no incomplete assemblies.\" (chainspot:experiments/parts-inspector/README.md:18-19) \u2014 a real PCR execution counted per Tick and per Calculation, exactly pyto's `PrincipleComponentRender -> Tick[]`, `Tick -> Calculation[]` shape (pyto/research/origin.md:20-23), running against ChainSpot's own algorithm rather than pyto's Python core.\n\nfeature-discovery/REVIEW.md documents materialization discipline as an explicit design choice: \"these timings do not measure deployed lazy inference; discovery intentionally materializes all features.\" (chainspot:packages/alg/src/stages/S1/exp/feature-discovery/REVIEW.md:21) \u2014 i.e. a discovery/inspection PCR is allowed to eagerly compute everything (unlike a production path), a distinction pyto's Tick materializer gap (tick-observability-ledger.md section 3) does not yet draw between an inspection run and a production run.\n\nbadge-assembly/REVIEW.md's unresolved-labels section names exactly the gaps pyto's own ledger names as missing from `pyto/src/pyto/`: \"{PartPublication}: canonical child addresses and sourceTickId stamping remain unset by the generic runner. Inspection follows actual nested output Parts and execution records.\" (chainspot:packages/alg/src/stages/S1/exp/badge-assembly/REVIEW.md:22) and \"{PartialExecutionRecords}: retain Calculation records completed before a failed Tick.\" (chainspot:packages/alg/src/stages/S1/exp/badge-assembly/REVIEW.md:24) \u2014 both are open problems in ChainSpot's own experimental S1, unresolved, not a solved reference pyto can simply copy. Variant/failure handling is stated as a design rule: \"Variant failures produce failed results and do not suppress later variants. Earlier writes in the failed fork remain available; partial Calculation records are not yet returned.\" (chainspot:packages/alg/src/stages/S1/exp/badge-assembly/REVIEW.md:13).\n\nThe docs/handoffs/BADGE-PCR-DISCOVERY-SCRIPT.md frames materialization order itself as a faithfulness requirement, not an implementation detail: \"Only after that live claim exists do the scratch PCR experiments subtract ownership and study the residue... Do not reorder these merely to make the diagram prettier. The useful PCR is the one so faithful to the actual algorithm that the missing counters become obvious accidentally.\" (chainspot:docs/handoffs/BADGE-PCR-DISCOVERY-SCRIPT.md:13,25) \u2014 a PCR's Tick order should mirror the real execution order closely enough that bugs surface as a byproduct of watching it, which is the origin.md founding need restated as a construction rule for the PCR author, not just the viewer.",
+  "receipts_and_telemetry": "This tip's receipts are still line-oriented text/JSON files, not a joined Tick-keyed structure \u2014 matching the exact gap tick-observability-ledger.md section 3 describes for pyto. docs/RATCHETS.md shows the concrete shape: a run writes `run.receipt.txt` with named `measurement <field> <value>` lines that a threshold-governance process reads back: \"Receipt: `artifacts/sweep/dev72-recovered-default/HeritagePark-full/run.receipt.txt`, `measurement padClaimDistancePx` (max=128.99) / `measurement padClaimMedianPx` (64.309) lines\" (chainspot:docs/RATCHETS.md:59). scripts/chainspot-lab/README.md documents the Sweep batch receipt shape precisely, including provenance-of-provenance: \"Each case writes normal Sweep evidence below `artifacts/sweep/<config>/batches/<course>/<case>/`; the batch root receives compact `summary.txt` and machine-readable `summary.json` receipts... Provenance appears in both receipts: badge/basket counts are accepted drawables from their named trace units... conformance drift counts receipts whose actual consumes/produces omit a declared slot.\" (chainspot:scripts/chainspot-lab/README.md:232) \u2014 \"conformance drift\" here is exactly pyto's `declared_consumes` vs `actual_consumes` divergence (pyto/research/tick-observability-ledger.md row 27), independently arrived at in ChainSpot's own Sweep receipts.\n\ndocs/abfeature-contract.md defines the telemetry primitives a Calculation-equivalent (\"unit\") must call, which is the clearest reusable API shape in this tip: \"**No silent drops**: if your code examines-and-rejects candidates, emit a rejected drawable with a `reason` per kill (`ctx.overlay(id, { type, \u2026, verdict: 'rejected', reason })`). This is the debuggability contract \u2014 'why 0 tees' must be answerable from the raster.\" (chainspot:docs/abfeature-contract.md:63-66) and \"Instrument what you measure: `ctx.measure(id, name, value)` (exact aggregates), `ctx.overlay(...)` (points/boxes/polylines in ORIGINAL-image px), `ctx.heatmap(id, key, float32)` for fields.\" (chainspot:docs/abfeature-contract.md:67-69). This `measure`/`overlay`/`heatmap` triad is a telemetry vocabulary pyto's Calculation authors could adopt almost verbatim for what a Part's \"value\" materializes into.\n\ndocs/unported/engine-dev72-lineage.md documents the origin of the \"no silent drops\" rule as a direct response to a real failure (\"the trophy-basket disaster\"): \"A tee candidate that failed a geometry window left no trace at all. The T3 comment in `measure.ts` states the goal exactly: 'no silent drops: every examined-and-killed candidate leaves a rejected drawable with its reason \u2014 this is the \"why 0 tees?\" answer on the raster.'\" (chainspot:docs/unported/engine-dev72-lineage.md:92-96) \u2014 receipts exist specifically to make negative outcomes (rejections), not just positive ones, inspectable; pyto's `origin.md` names the same failure mode generically (\"agents then produced dumb receipts and self-verified,\" pyto/research/origin.md:14-15) but does not yet have this rejection-with-reason convention anywhere in `pyto/src/pyto/`.",
