+  "addressing": "No explicit \"root\", \"mount\", or \"the point is connection\" statement exists anywhere in this tip -- that framing is a later, pyto-side/owner concept and is absent here. Report as missing rather than inferred. What the tip does have is a working, disciplined dotted address space:\n\n- SlotRef is kept a plain open string on purpose so finer-grained decomposition never requires a schema change: contract.ts:16-25 -- \"Deliberately a plain string, not a closed enum: R2 requires operations at a finer grain than the existing engine units, so the slot namespace has to stay open (e.g. 'badgeStage.brightMask', 'badgeStage.components', 'assignment.scoring', 'assignment.selection') without this file growing every time a unit gets decomposed further.\" Legality (who-produces-before-who-consumes) is enforced by the compiler's dependency walk, never by the type system (contract.ts:22-24).\n- Calculation identities are constrained to a `fn.` prefix at the type level: PxFn.address is typed `` `fn.${string}` `` (board.ts:18), and PQL enforces it at parse time -- pql.ts:60: \"if (!call.startsWith('fn.')) throw new Error(`PQL ${where}.call: expected a registered fn. address.`)\".\n- Part/value addresses observed in receipts and the parts-inspector use a dotted domain.stage.component convention, not a generic root: `px.course.canonicalPixels` (+page.svelte:26), `px.image.cropped` / `px.components.masks` / `px.components` (componentPxC.ts:12-15), and `px.s1.exp.maskComponents.part.blackComponent-115` (receipt.json candidates row) -- each segment names a real domain object, never a placeholder.\n- A run itself gets addressed under a `px.pql.` prefix keyed by the composition's own declared name: pql.ts:108 -- `pxc.set(\\`px.pql.${composition.PrincipleComponentRender}\\`, run);` -- i.e. the \"namespace root\" that exists in this tip is `px.pql.<name>` for run records specifically, chosen by the PCR's own title rather than assigned generically.\n- PxC itself is described as \"PxC's browser-safe address space\" (board.ts:37-41), with `fork()` for isolated snapshots and `register`/`call` keeping calculation identity distinct from Part storage (board.ts:42-50).\n\nNo mounts, no per-namespace backend selection, and no discussion of what makes a root \"meaningful\" appear in this tip; the owner's later \"connection\" framing is not represented here at all.",
+  "viewer_and_pcr_render": "This tip contains a working, rendering answer to \"PCRs are Kibana\" that predates the phrase. Two surfaces:\n\n1. `TickInspection.svelte` (src/lib/evidence-workbench/TickInspection.svelte), a Storybook-facing per-Tick panel. Per Tick it renders: an identity strip of \"Run Args identity\" (`pcr.paramsHash`), \"Plan\" (`pcr.planFingerprint`), \"Run result\" (`pcr.runResultId`) plus the honesty line `{pcr.runResultIdentityScope} -- {pcr.runResultIdentityLimitation}` (TickInspection.svelte:40-56); a three-column flow of \"Exact PxC inputs\" (`tick.actualConsumes`, flagging any declared-but-unread address), \"Calculation identity\" (`tick.frozenCalculations`, each showing `calculation.address`, its SHA-256 `implementationHash`, its `identityScope`, and the explicit non-transitivity warning `calculation.limitation`), and \"Exact PxC outputs\" (`tick.writes`, each address plus its `kind`, with `replacement` writes visually flagged) (TickInspection.svelte:58-97); a \"Materializations emitted by this Tick\" section listing each artifact's kind/id/sha256/dims, or else the explicit line \"No persisted Materialization at this boundary. PxC outputs remain inspectable by address.\" when none exist (TickInspection.svelte:100-119); and a \"Residue / UNKNOWN\" section that is either populated with named unexplained-pixel counts or shows \"UNKNOWN -- this Tick establishes no molecule-specific residue account.\" (TickInspection.svelte:122-133).\n\n2. `PcrInspectionHost.svelte` composes several PCRs/specimens over that per-Tick panel and computes a `renderId` via `pcrRenderId(pcr, {materializationView, zoom, showGrid, showReceipt, specimenId})` (PcrInspectionHost.svelte:72-76), with the header note \"Projection-only MaterializationView -- View Args never cross the production gateway\" (PcrInspectionHost.svelte:107-109). `pcr.ts:90-93` defines that function with the comment \"View identity is a pure projection of a frozen PCR; it cannot execute CV.\" `pcr.ts:18-21` states the governing rule directly: \"Storybook-facing composition of already-executed Tick testimony. PCR has deliberately no run() method: the production gateway is the only execution authority and hands this function the receipts it already produced.\"\n\nA second, separate viewer is the DuckDB experiment's own `/parts` page (src/staging-routes/parts/+page.svelte): it seeds PxC with a saved S0 raster, runs the S1 YAML once, then renders `QueryPanel` (SQL presets over the projected run) beside `PartInspector` (the raw pixel Parts), so a person sees a value (the pixels) fed by a query that selected it -- but this view is keyed by SQL result row / Part identity, not by Tick, and shows no Calculation identity or write-kind at all; it complements TickInspection rather than replacing it.",
+  "storage": "DuckDB-Wasm is the backend under test, and the tip is explicit about the boundary it drew and why it failed the budget:\n\n- `projectRun` (src/lib/parts-query/projection.ts:64-144) walks a completed `PqlRun` and produces three scalar-only tables -- `results` (2,163 rows: one per Calculation output item, with tick/call/output_address/collection/label/id/kind/verdict/status/reason), `parts` (1,130 rows: one per distinct component/mask, with id/kind/pixel_count/bbox/polarity/label/bbox_source_result), and `result_parts` (2,390 rows: result-to-part occurrence with a `role_path`). The doc comment states the boundary: \"Project a completed PQL run into scalar DuckDB tables. Pixel buffers stay in PxC and are only measured.\" (projection.ts:63).\n- `tables.ts` loads these into DuckDB inside one transaction with escaped SQL literals and batched 200-row inserts (tables.ts:12-35); `database.ts` opens one disposable in-memory DuckDB-Wasm instance per completed run via `openRunDatabase`, with the comment \"One disposable database per completed run. PxC remains the source of the actual Parts.\" (database.ts:9).\n- Content addressing is present at two layers: `FrozenCalculation.implementationHash` is \"SHA-256 of the bound executable function body in this runtime build\" with an honest scope tag `identityScope: 'runtime-function-body'` and limitation \"called helpers, constants, templates, and assets are not covered\" (contract.ts:60-68); and `ArtifactRef` is a \"Content-addressed reference to an artifact the sink has stored\" carrying `sha256`, a sink-defined opaque `uri`, and optional `dims` (contract.ts:90-113). The DuckDB experiment's own receipt.json records `sourceRgbaSha256` and `compositionSha256` for the exact run it queried.\n- What was archived and why, verbatim from REPORT.md: \"One final Node run measured S1 at 613 ms, projection at 46 ms, and database initialization plus table loading at 3.80 s... The browser preview measured startup at 5.79 s... The exception-handling Wasm asset is 34,242,586 bytes uncompressed... DuckDB adds substantial startup weight for this small dataset. No CV speedup was attempted or demonstrated.\" The report's own responsibility boundary: \"PxC and PQL retain execution and original material. No core store, registry, executor, or YAML edits were required... Projection reads a completed run. SQL holds scalar records and identity links; raw pixel buffers stay with the Parts... DuckDB owns relational query execution... This is deliberately a single completed-run query surface. It does not provide live subscriptions, cross-run versioning, a general SQL editor, arbitrary PxC enumeration, or execution optimization.\"\n- No StorageKinds-style \"backend chosen by address prefix\" exists in this tip; DuckDB was evaluated as a single global projection layer over one run, not as a namespace-scoped backend, and it was rejected outright rather than scoped down.",
+  "materialization_and_ticks": "`materialize` is one of exactly five allowed OperationKind verbs: \"'measure' | 'transform' | 'compute' | 'decide' | 'materialize'\" (contract.ts:13). A `Materialization` is formally an `ArtifactRef` -- id, kind, sha256, uri, optional dims (contract.ts:90-113) -- handed to an injected sink; the exec core \"imports nothing and reaches no I/O\" (contract.ts:1-10), so materializing is deliberately kept outside the pure execution core.\n\nThe most load-bearing finding for pyto's `{?} CacheInvalidation` is `Pcr`'s own stated identity limitation: `runResultIdentityScope: 'tick-testimony-and-materializations'` and `runResultIdentityLimitation: 'opaque PxC output values require an emitted Materialization'` (pcr.ts:29-31). In other words: a PCR's content-addressed `runResultId` is a hash over Tick testimony only (pcr.ts:70-76); an opaque PxC value that was never explicitly materialized is NOT covered by that identity at all. `TickInspection.svelte` makes this gap visible to a person rather than hiding it: when `tick.artifacts` is empty it prints \"No persisted Materialization at this boundary. PxC outputs remain inspectable by address.\" (TickInspection.svelte:115-119) -- i.e. the UI names the exact condition under which run identity does not reach a value.\n\n`materializeBadgeSpecimens.mjs` (src/lib/server/evidence-workbench/materializeBadgeSpecimens.mjs) is the Node-side materializer that actually runs a real detector pipeline (`compileExecutionPlan` -> `executeCompiledPlan` -> `composePcr`) against a real or corpus image and decodes each artifact kind into Storybook-ready fixtures, falling back to an explicit \"unavailable\" specimen (with the note \"Real E materialization unavailable: ... does not exist. Set CHAINSPOT_BADGE_IMAGE or place chainspot-corpus beside ChainSpot.\") when no real input exists (materializeBadgeSpecimens.mjs:37-51) -- a concrete pattern for \"declare the gap rather than fabricate a value\" that pyto's own generate_returns.py citation-or-fail rule already mirrors independently.\n\nThe `matrix-review/render.py` and `matrix-review/gallery.py` files that `tick-observability-ledger.md` row 7 marks **missing** (\"filesystem-wide search found neither file\") are present in this archived tip at `experiments/dashs-track-edge-sensing/matrix-review/{render.py,gallery.py}` (with a `proof/` directory of receipts and comparison PNGs alongside them) -- the ledger's gap is filled by this branch, not by anything on main.",
+  "receipts_and_telemetry": "The `Receipt` shape (== `TickTestimony`, contract.ts:127-148) is: `opId`, `frozenCalculations: FrozenCalculation[]`, `startedAtMs`, `durationMs`, `declaredConsumes`/`declaredProduces`, `actualConsumes`/`actualProduces`, `writes: PxWriteTestimony[]`, `probes: Probe[]`, `artifacts: ArtifactRef[]`. Two fields are more granular than anything documented yet in pyto:\n\n- `PxWriteTestimony` is `{address, kind: 'new-address' | 'refinement' | 'replacement'}` (contract.ts:71-74), and the doc comment on `Receipt.writes` states the policy directly: \"Collision-visible PxC writes; replacements are never hidden as ordinary output.\" (contract.ts:137). `trackAccess` in board.ts computes this kind live by checking `board.has(slot)` and whether the address was in the Tick's own `declaredConsumes` before the write (board.ts:119-131) -- a three-way richer signal than pyto's current binary \"hit\" (`{?} HitDefinition`).\n- `Probe` is a tiny named-telemetry primitive distinct from writes or artifacts: `{name: string, value: number}` (contract.ts:115-119), described as \"A single named numeric observation captured during an operation's run\" -- exactly the kind of cheap scalar telemetry pyto's Receipt today has no field for at all (`tick-observability-ledger.md` section 3, item 3: \"Receipt has no value field\").\n- `accessConformance?: 'exact'` (contract.ts:51) is an opt-in strict mode a `Tick`/OperationSpec can declare; `gateway.ts:117` throws \"PxC: Tick '...' access contradicts its declaration; ...\" when actual and declared consumes/produces diverge under that mode -- a stricter, characterized answer to pyto's open `{?} SilentRules` (whether pcr.py should raise at declaration time).\n- The actual DuckDB experiment's `receipt.json` (both the duckdb-inspection and parts-inspector variants) shows the concrete telemetry shape in practice: `sourceCommit`, `sourceImage`, `sourceRgbaSha256`, `compositionSha256`, `engine`, `engineRuntime`, `executionMs`/`projectionMs`/`databaseMs`, `ticks`, `calculations`, `tables: {results, parts, result_parts}`, `candidates`, `incomplete`, `selectedPlates`, `rejectedPlates`, `queries: [{id, title, sql, elapsedMs, rows}]`, `selectedCandidate`, `selectedTarget`, `selectedParts`, `checks`, `browserVerified` -- durations and digests sit beside literal row data in one file, unlike pyto's current split across `testimony.json`/`receipts.json`/`retained.json`.\n- A telemetry-discipline warning surfaces twice in `docs/unported/g3-intact-tee-family.md` (lines 108 and 244): a diagnostic-only scalar (`grayCount`) that was only ever `console.log`'d must never become a decision input -- \"LAB gray payload (145 <= max(R,G,B) <= 175) was DIAGNOSTIC ONLY. It never selected/rejected this family. Do not add a gray kill rule.\" This is a live, named case of the exact discipline pyto's `probes`/`Observation` split should preserve: cheap named numbers are for people to read, never for a Calculation to branch on silently.",
