+  "addressing": "Two address families, both plain strings, not closed enums, and both dotted namespaces meant to discriminate. (1) SlotRef / px.* Part addresses: \"Deliberately a plain string, not a closed enum: R2 requires operations at a finer grain than the existing engine units, so the slot namespace has to stay open (e.g. 'badgeStage.brightMask', 'badgeStage.components', 'assignment.scoring', 'assignment.selection') without this file growing every time a unit gets decomposed further.\" (5607611:packages/alg/src/exec/contract.ts:15-22). In practice these are dotted hierarchies that read as a path from Stage to sub-part: `px.image.cropped`, `px.components.masks`, `px.components` (5607611:packages/alg/src/stages/componentPxC.ts:12-16), and inside the S1 experiment `px.s1.exp.maskComponents.part.blackMask`, `px.s1.exp.badgeAssembly.plates` etc. (5607611:packages/alg/src/stages/S1/exp/badge-assembly/PrincipleComponentRender.yaml:7,12,53). (2) fn.* Calculation addresses, same discipline, checked at parse time: \"if (!call.startsWith('fn.')) throw new Error(`PQL ${where}.call: expected a registered fn. address.`)\" (5607611:packages/alg/src/exec/pql.ts:60), giving addresses like `fn.s1.exp.badgeAssembly.selectComponents` (5607611:packages/alg/src/stages/S1/exp/badge-assembly/index.ts:110-112). There is no single global \"root\" segment or generic top-level label anywhere in this address space \u2014 every first segment already names a real thing (`px`, `fn`) and every subsequent segment narrows (stage id, `exp`, sub-experiment name, part role), which is the closest concrete precedent in this tip to the owner's \"a meaningless root is wasted log(N) potential\" rule: nothing here spends a segment on a label that doesn't discriminate. Runs are addressed the same way: the PQL run record itself is written back into the store at `px.pql.<PrincipleComponentRender-id>` \u2014 \"pxc.set(`px.pql.${composition.PrincipleComponentRender}`, run);\" (5607611:packages/alg/src/exec/pql.ts:108) \u2014 and a Stage's set of variant results at `px.pql.<Stage>.results` (5607611:packages/alg/src/exec/stage.ts:43; corroborated in prose at 5607611:packages/alg/src/stages/S1/exp/badge-assembly/REVIEW.md:12: \"The caller receives results and the input PxC stores them at px.pql.<Stage>.results.\"). \"Mount\"/\"namespace\" as pyto's `{?} StorageKinds`/`{?} PerAppBudget` use those words (a backend chosen per address prefix or namespace) is NOT present in this tip: no code here selects a storage backend by address prefix, and grep for \"mount\" across the diff's exec/stage code returns nothing \u2014 the only per-address dispatch implemented is `Part` role (mask/component/scalar) inferred structurally at render time (see viewer section), not a declared storage kind. So this tip advances the *shape* of addresses (open dotted strings, `px.`/`fn.` prefixes, no closed registry) but does not resolve `{?} StorageKinds` or the mounts question at all.",
+  "viewer_and_pcr_render": "Per-Tick, per-Calculation, with values, not just addresses \u2014 this is the ChainSpot-side answer to the pyto tick-observability-ledger's \u00a73 gap (\"No function in pyto/src/pyto/ consumes a PcrRun\"). `invokePql` executes a `PqlComposition` (Ticks -> Calculations, each `call`/`with`/`args`/`into`) synchronously in declaration order and records, per Calculation, `actualCall`, resolved `inputs`, `args`, and `output` \"held by reference rather than copied\" (5607611:packages/alg/src/exec/pql.ts:14-31,83-109 \u2014 comment at line 20-22). `renderPqlHtml` turns that `PqlRun` into a self-contained, dependency-free HTML page: \"A portable inspector over actual Calculation outputs, with no detector calls.\" (5607611:packages/alg/src/exec/render.ts:25). It builds a per-operation dropdown (`${c.tick} / ${c.call}`), a details pane dumping `{tick, call, args, into}` as JSON, and a pixel-layer SVG view via `pixelLayers()`, which walks the output value structurally and dispatches on a `.kind` tag: `if(node.kind==='cropped-raster')return;` / `if(node.kind==='component'||node.kind==='mask'){...}` (5607611:packages/alg/src/exec/render.ts:67-71) \u2014 i.e., Part *kind* is inferred from the value's own tag at render time, not declared on the address (contrast with pyto's `{?}` gap #4 in tick-observability-ledger.md: \"No way to tell an image Part from a scalar Part... There is no kind, dtype, or renderer tag\"). `renderPartsSvg` composites one `<path>` per pixel layer with run-length-encoded rows into one SVG, with an \"Export current SVG\" button. The REVIEW.md is explicit that this is descriptive and not a scoring tool: \"View controls perform no CV calculations.\" (5607611:packages/alg/src/stages/S1/exp/badge-assembly/REVIEW.md:14). PCR itself (`composePcr` in pcr.ts) is a separate, narrower artifact: a frozen, content-hashed *identity* over already-executed Tick testimony for Storybook, explicitly refusing to be an execution surface \u2014 \"PCR has deliberately no run() method: the production gateway is the only execution authority and hands this function the receipts it already produced.\" (5607611:packages/alg/src/exec/pcr.ts:17-20). It computes `runResultId = sha256(planFingerprint, paramsHash, frozenTickIdentity(tick)[])` and separately `pcrRenderId(pcr, viewArgs)` as \"a pure projection of a frozen PCR; it cannot execute CV\" (5607611:packages/alg/src/exec/pcr.ts:90-92). It self-declares the scope limit pyto also hit: \"Explicitly bounded: opaque PxC values are not silently claimed as content-frozen.\" / `runResultIdentityLimitation: 'opaque PxC output values require an emitted Materialization'` (5607611:packages/alg/src/exec/pcr.ts:29-31) \u2014 the same value-vs-digest gap tick-observability-ledger.md documents for pyto's `Receipt.result_sha256`.",
+  "storage": "No DuckDB and no specialized-backend-by-prefix code anywhere in this tip (grep across the diff for \"duckdb\" is empty); the storage story here is entirely in-memory PxC plus one concrete content-addressing precedent. PxC (\"PxC's browser-safe address space\") is a plain in-memory Map-backed store: `export function createExecBoard(): PxC { return boardFrom(new Map(), new Map()); }` (5607611:packages/alg/src/exec/board.ts:52,54), with `get`/`has`/`set`/`register`/`call` and a `fork()` that \"snapshots slot and function maps... shares immutable values, including raster buffers. Each run's writes stay in its own fork.\" (5607611:packages/alg/src/exec/board.ts:44 comment; corroborated in prose at 5607611:packages/alg/src/stages/S1/exp/badge-assembly/REVIEW.md:11). This is the ABFeature-tournament mechanism: `createPqlStage` forks PxC once per variant (Default + each registered feature), runs the same composition against each fork with calculation overrides, and keeps every fork's failed and successful writes (5607611:packages/alg/src/exec/stage.ts:25-41; REVIEW.md:13 \"Variant failures produce failed results and do not suppress later variants. Earlier writes in the failed fork remain available\"). Content addressing exists as a real, shipped convention one layer up (G0 intake, not PxC itself): \"canonical composite materialization: real pixel flattening (Node-runnable, no canvas) + content-addressed `imageId` = sha256 over `[u32be widthPx][u32be heightPx][raw RGBA bytes]`, documented and pinned by a known-tiny-composite unit test.\" (5607611:.task/CHSPT-82.md:269-272). Artifacts extracted during a Tick run are also content-addressed at the sink boundary: `ArtifactRef` carries `sha256` and a sink-defined `uri`, \"a file:// path from the Node sink, an in-memory handle from a browser/collector sink) \u2014 the exec core never interprets it, only carries it through the receipt.\" (5607611:packages/alg/src/exec/contract.ts:90-108, comment at 90-95). Nothing in this tip answers pyto's `{?} StorageKinds` (backend chosen by address prefix or declared Part kind) \u2014 there is exactly one backend (the in-memory Map board) and one content-addressing convention (image sha256), not a facade over several.",
+  "materialization_and_ticks": "\"Materialize\" is used with a specific, disciplined meaning: a materialization is PCR-only render output, never written back to PxC as a Part. S1's contract explicitly states the rule in its own receipt text: `'materialization: subtraction rasters are PCR output only; they are not written to PxC'` (5607611:packages/alg/src/stages/S1/contract.ts:24), produced by `materializeS1Subtractions(run)` (same file, line 9) which derives display rasters (BadgePx subtraction, Badge mute subtraction panels) from the already-computed `run` without mutating the PxC the run returned. This is the same distinction pyto's tick-observability-ledger.md draws between `PcrRun.results` (values, live in memory) and everything serialized (digest-only) \u2014 here ChainSpot keeps the derived pixels entirely outside the addressable store on purpose, rather than under-specifying where they'd live. Tick-as-synchronization-boundary is enforced structurally, not just documented: `executeCompiledPlan` walks `plan.ops` in order, wraps the board once per operation with `trackAccess` (recording `consumed`/`produced`/`writes` for exactly that op), runs the implementation, times it (`durationMs = now() - startedAtMs`), and only then asserts `assertAccessConformance` when `op.accessConformance === 'exact'`, throwing on any declared/actual read or write mismatch \u2014 \"PxC: Tick '${op.id}' access contradicts its declaration; missing reads=[...] extra reads=[...] missing writes=[...] extra writes=[...].\" (5607611:packages/alg/src/exec/gateway.ts:103-122, throw text at 116-120). `trackAccess` is the mechanism, wrapping (not replacing) the board: \"Wraps a board so every get()/set() call made through the wrapper during one operation's run is recorded \u2014 the gateway's source for `actualConsumes`/`actualProduces` on that operation's Receipt.\" (5607611:packages/alg/src/exec/board.ts:82-86). A synchronous and an async gateway variant exist (`executeCompiledPlan` / `executeCompiledPlanAsync`), both producing the identical `Receipt` shape, which is exactly the \"JS as reference semantics, node vs browser\" split pyto's Reframing 4 calls for.",
+  "receipts_and_telemetry": "Receipt shape (5607611:packages/alg/src/exec/contract.ts:127-141): `opId`, `frozenCalculations` (address + implementation body sha256, honestly scoped \u2014 \"Honest scope of implementationHash. It is not a transitive source or dependency hash.\" line 65-67), `startedAtMs`/`durationMs`, `declaredConsumes`/`declaredProduces` vs `actualConsumes`/`actualProduces`, `writes: readonly PxWriteTestimony[]` where each write is classified `'new-address' | 'refinement' | 'replacement'` based on whether the address existed before and whether it was declared as consumed \u2014 \"Collision-visible PxC writes; replacements are never hidden as ordinary output.\" (line 137, classification logic at 5607611:packages/alg/src/exec/board.ts:119-131), `probes: readonly Probe[]` (generic `{name, value}` shape-probes: \"for every slot an operation actually produced, record its element count when the value is array-like\" \u2014 5607611:packages/alg/src/exec/gateway.ts:83-101), and `artifacts: readonly ArtifactRef[]` (`{id, kind, sha256, uri, dims?}`). `TickTestimony` is literally `type TickTestimony = Receipt` (5607611:packages/alg/src/exec/contract.ts:148), i.e. this tip's Tick and Receipt are the same object \u2014 there is no separate testimony-vs-receipt split the way pyto's `TickTestimony`/`Receipt` are two structures joined by invocation id; pyto's tick-observability-ledger.md's \"No Tick\u2194receipt join\" gap (\u00a73 item 2) does not exist in this ChainSpot design because there was never a second object to join. `PCR.frozenTickIdentity` strips a tick's testimony down to `{opId, frozenCalculations, actualConsumes, actualProduces, writes, artifacts}` (dims and value-bearing fields explicitly excluded) before hashing it into `runResultId` (5607611:packages/alg/src/exec/pcr.ts:35-49) \u2014 the digest is over structure/identity, never over opaque Part values, matching pyto's `Receipt.result_sha256` boundary. `ABFeatureSetManifest` layers a set-level telemetry record on top of per-op Receipts: `manifestHash` is computed over a `semanticReceipt` that deliberately excludes wall-clock fields \u2014 \"Deterministic semantic receipt hash; wall-clock fields are intentionally excluded.\" (5607611:packages/alg/src/exec/feature-set.ts:113, exclusion at 345-349: `operations.map(({startedAtMs, durationMs, ...receipt}) => {void startedAtMs; void durationMs; return receipt;})`) \u2014 a concrete instance of separating a byte-stable identity channel from a timing/telemetry channel, which is exactly pyto's own `{?} ObservationSeam` design (digests/durations off to the side so consumer bytes stay stable). `formatABFeatureSetManifestMarkdown` (5607611:packages/alg/src/exec/feature-set.ts:367-409) is a plain-text/markdown receipt report per operation (time, declared/actual consumes/produces, probes, artifacts) \u2014 the nearest thing in this tip to pyto's `comparison.md`.",
